-- ===== Retroactive knowledge box: q_tn_facial_pain_001 =====
update questions
set data = data || '{"explanation":"<p><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Cluster headache is a trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia (TAC), which is associated with autonomic features (e.g. lacrimation, nasal congestion, ptosis) absent in this case.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Trigeminal neuropathic pain presents as a constant pain, and examination will reveal negative and positive neurological signs — the normal examination here makes this diagnosis unlikely.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Paroxysmal hemicrania is a TAC, which is also associated with autonomic features absent in this case.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> TMJ syndrome causes continuous pain that worsens with chewing; examination typically reveals masseter tenderness — neither feature is present here.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Correct.</strong> Trigeminal neuralgia (TN) presents as short-lasting, lancinating, electric shock-like paroxysms in one or more branches of the trigeminal nerve, triggered by light stimuli (eating, talking, touch). The pain-free interval between attacks and a normal neurological examination are characteristic. First-line treatment is pharmacological (carbamazepine or oxcarbazepine). Surgical options — including microvascular decompression — are considered when medical treatment fails, particularly in the presence of a neurovascular conflict on MRI.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Trigeminal Neuralgia</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Definition</div><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">A unilateral painful disorder characterised by <strong>brief, electric shock-like pains</strong>, abrupt in onset and termination, limited to the distribution of one or more divisions of the trigeminal nerve.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Epidemiology</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Incidence / prevalence</th><td>Annual incidence 3&ndash;5/100,000; worldwide prevalence 10&ndash;300/100,000</td></tr><tr><th>Age</th><td><strong>Peak age of onset 50&ndash;60 years</strong>; rare under 40 years</td></tr><tr><th>Sex</th><td><strong>Male:female ratio 1:2</strong> &mdash; women affected almost twice as often</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Aetiology and Pathophysiology</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Exact mechanism unclear; the leading model is the <strong>&ldquo;ignition theory&rdquo;</strong> &mdash; abnormal hyperexcitability of afferent neurones in the trigeminal root/ganglion following injury</td></tr><tr><td>Most cases are attributed to <strong>demyelination at the root entry zone</strong>, where Schwann cell (peripheral) myelin meets astrocyte (central) myelin, near the pons</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ephaptic cross-talk</strong>: demyelinated A&beta; (touch) fibres lying close to A&delta;/C (pain) fibres allow trivial tactile stimulation to trigger pain</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Vascular compression</strong> (typically by the <strong>superior cerebellar artery</strong>) is the most commonly implicated cause of demyelination, supported by surgical/MRI evidence &mdash; although vascular contact is an inconsistent finding and is also seen incidentally in people without TN</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Risk factors</strong>: multiple sclerosis, increasing age, stroke, hypertension (in women), Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, tumours near the trigeminal root</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MS-associated TN</strong> presents at a <strong>younger age</strong> and tolerates anticonvulsants less well, but responds to interventions similarly to classical TN</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Classification</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Type</th><th>Definition</th><th>Subtypes</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Classical TN</strong></td><td>No apparent cause other than possible neurovascular compression</td><td><strong>Purely paroxysmal</strong> &mdash; pain-free between attacks<br><strong>With concomitant persistent facial pain</strong> (atypical TN / TN type 2) &mdash; low-grade background pain persists; central sensitisation implicated; neurovascular compression often not demonstrable; more treatment-resistant</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Idiopathic TN</strong></td><td>No apparent cause, with no neurovascular compression</td><td><strong>Purely paroxysmal</strong><br><strong>With concomitant persistent facial pain</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Secondary TN</strong></td><td>Caused by an identifiable disorder &mdash; MS, herpes zoster, trauma, space-occupying lesion</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Diagnostic Criteria (NICE CKS, Adapted from ICHD)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>A</th><td>Paroxysmal attacks lasting a fraction of a second to 2 minutes, in one or more trigeminal divisions, fulfilling B and C</td></tr><tr><th>B</th><td>Pain has at least one of: (1) intense, sharp, superficial or stabbing quality; (2) precipitated from trigger areas or by trigger factors</td></tr><tr><th>C</th><td>Attacks are stereotyped in the individual patient</td></tr><tr><th>D</th><td>No clinically evident neurological deficit</td></tr><tr><th>E</th><td>Not attributed to another disorder</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Red Flags Suggesting Secondary TN</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Red flag</th></tr><tr><td>Sensory deficit, absent trigeminal reflexes, bilateral involvement</td></tr><tr><td>Younger age of onset (&lt;40 years)</td></tr><tr><td>Pain that is persistent rather than paroxysmal</td></tr><tr><td>Difficult-to-control pain</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Differential Diagnosis</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Headache disorders</th><td>Cluster headache, other trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias</td></tr><tr><th>Dental pain</th><td>Cracked tooth, dental abscess</td></tr><tr><th>Joint</th><td>Temporomandibular joint disorder</td></tr><tr><th>Other neuralgias</th><td>Occipital, glossopharyngeal, post-herpetic neuralgia; atypical facial pain</td></tr><tr><th>Tumours</th><td>Acoustic neuroma, meningioma</td></tr><tr><th>Other</th><td>Sinusitis</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Investigations</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Primarily a <strong>clinical diagnosis</strong> &mdash; no investigation is required to confirm it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MRI brain</strong> identifies a secondary cause in up to 15% of patients, and high-resolution MRI may identify vascular compression; recommended particularly where red flags are present, and NICE CKS advises specialist referral for MRI assessment in general</td></tr><tr><td>Neurological examination to detect deficit, trigger areas and laterality</td></tr><tr><td>Evoked potentials / quantitative sensory testing / electrophysiology &mdash; insufficient evidence for routine use</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-operative MR tomographic angiography may aid patient selection and outcome prediction for MVD</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Relevant Anatomy</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>CN V is the <strong>largest cranial nerve</strong> &mdash; mixed sensory and motor</td></tr><tr><td>The sensory root forms the <strong>trigeminal (Gasserian) ganglion</strong> within <strong>Meckel&rsquo;s cave</strong> (middle cranial fossa), dividing into V1 (ophthalmic), V2 (maxillary) and V3 (mandibular)</td></tr><tr><td>Exits: <strong>V1</strong> via the superior orbital fissure; <strong>V2</strong> via the foramen rotundum; <strong>V3</strong> via the foramen ovale</td></tr><tr><td>The motor root travels with the sensory root but distributes <strong>only via V3</strong>, supplying the muscles of mastication</td></tr><tr><td>Parasympathetic fibres are carried via trigeminal branches to the lacrimal gland (V1/V2), nasal glands (V2) and submandibular/sublingual/parotid glands (V3)</td></tr></table><div style=\"text-align:center;margin-top:12px\"><img src=\"images/trigeminal-nerve-divisions.svg\" alt=\"The trigeminal nerve: sensory root from the pons to the Gasserian ganglion in Meckel&rsquo;s cave, dividing into V1 (superior orbital fissure), V2 (foramen rotundum) and V3 (foramen ovale), with the motor root joining V3, and the three levels targeted by surgery\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:4px;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\"></div></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Pharmacological Management</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Role</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Carbamazepine</strong></td><td><strong>First-line</strong></td><td><strong>NNT 1.8</strong>; blocks use-dependent sodium channels and may prevent synaptic transmission in the trigeminal nucleus. Start 100 mg once or twice daily and titrate; usual dose 200 mg 3&ndash;4 times daily, max 1.6 g/day. Side effects: drowsiness, dizziness, rash, liver damage, hyponatraemia, ataxia &mdash; monitor LFTs, FBC and electrolytes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Oxcarbazepine</strong></td><td>Alternative</td><td>Carbamazepine derivative; better side-effect profile, similar efficacy</td></tr><tr><td>Gabapentin, pregabalin, amitriptyline</td><td>Commonly used</td><td>Efficacy specific to TN not strongly evidenced (the NICE neuropathic pain pathway explicitly excludes TN)</td></tr><tr><td>Baclofen, lamotrigine</td><td>Add-on therapy</td><td>Baclofen may help in MS-associated TN</td></tr><tr><td>Phenytoin</td><td>Limited benefit</td><td>Used in some cases</td></tr><tr><td>Opioids</td><td>Not recommended</td><td>Ineffective for paroxysmal TN pain; NICE advises against starting them in non-specialist settings</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">If carbamazepine is ineffective, not tolerated or contraindicated &rarr; seek specialist advice / early referral, rather than cycling through other non-specific analgesics.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Surgical Management (Failed or Intolerant of Pharmacotherapy)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Procedure</th><th>Outcome</th><th>Risks</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Peripheral</strong></td><td>Branch neurolysis &mdash; laser, alcohol, neurectomy</td><td>Short-term relief (6 months&ndash;1 year); useful if unfit for other surgery, or as an emergency procedure</td><td>Dysaesthesia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gasserian ganglion</strong> (percutaneous, via foramen ovale under fluoroscopy)</td><td>Thermal (radiofrequency), chemical (glycerol/phenol/alcohol) or mechanical (balloon compression) ablation</td><td>Relief for ~4&ndash;5 years in 50% of patients</td><td>High incidence of sensory loss/dysaesthesia; ~4% develop <strong>anaesthesia dolorosa</strong>. Balloon compression specifically: arrhythmias, aseptic meningitis, temporary diplopia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Posterior fossa</strong></td><td><strong>Microvascular decompression (MVD)</strong> &mdash; suboccipital craniotomy; the vessel (typically the superior cerebellar artery) is separated from the nerve root with Teflon felt</td><td><strong>Best initial success rate (80&ndash;90%)</strong> and the most sustained relief of all procedures; 10-year relapse ~30&ndash;40%</td><td>Mortality ~0.5% (major neurosurgery); aseptic meningitis (11%), hearing loss (1%), sensory loss (7%), CSF leak, haematoma, infarcts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Posterior fossa</strong></td><td><strong>Gamma knife stereotactic radiosurgery</strong> &mdash; focused radiation to the nerve root where vascular compression is confirmed on MRI mapping</td><td>Complete relief in up to 69% by 1 year, but benefit may not be sustained; can be repeated; an option for patients unsuitable for MVD</td><td>Facial numbness, paraesthesia</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Psychological and Other Interventions</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Multidisciplinary input</strong>, including psychology, is valuable given the significant impact on wellbeing; CBT and ACT are considered, although the evidence quality is limited</td></tr><tr><td>Neuromodulation (trigeminal, occipital, peripheral nerve, spinal cord, motor cortex stimulation) &mdash; not yet robust enough for routine use</td></tr><tr><td>Non-invasive repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation &mdash; benefit shown in orofacial pain generally; role in TN specifically not established</td></tr><tr><td>Botulinum toxin type A &mdash; case reports/open studies only; larger trials needed</td></tr><tr><td>TENS, acupuncture, 5% lidocaine patches &mdash; tried with varying results</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb
where id = 'q_tn_facial_pain_001';
